    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the repo, and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: `membershipTier` becomes `GOLD` when the owner's household (owners sharing the same `householdId`) reaches 3+ members after creation; otherwise the existing BRONZE/SILVER rules apply.\n\n**Implementation** \u2014 a new, self-contained aspect `OwnerGoldMembershipAdvice` rather than editing the existing tier logic:\n\n- `householdId` is derived from the normalized (case- and whitespace-insensitive) last name + address, so two owners share a household iff those keys match. The aspect counts matching owners and sets `GOLD` when `>= 3`.\n- It advises the same `addOwner`/`getOwner` join points as the existing `OwnerNamesakeCountAdvice` (which sets BRONZE/SILVER), and uses `@Order(0)` so its after-returning advice runs *last*, letting GOLD override the base tier. Only owners with a real household (non-null last name and address) are eligible.\n\n**Why a new class kept structural impact low:** `membershipTier` had a single writer, but adding the household logic there would have made every new method pay the WMC of that class's existing complex method. A fresh, cohesive aspect has `WMC_other \u2248 1`, and each of its small methods stays simple \u2014 one file touched, no existing function grown.\n\nOne correction during development: `@Order(HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)` runs before Spring's `ExposeInvocationInterceptor` and broke AOP with an `IllegalStateException`; `@Order(0)` still outranks the unordered namesake aspect without that problem.",
